[Event "Vienna: the set up"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/9NYe5QGB"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Welcome to the Vienna: a quiet knight to c3 first, then the f pawn flies at his king. Open with the king pawn. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { Your pawn claims the centre from e4, the same first step as countless other openings. } (1. d4 { The queen pawn opening is just as strong, but it leads somewhere else entirely. } 1... d5 2. c4 e6 { That is the Queens Gambit, sound chess with real history behind it. The Vienna only exists once the king pawn leads. }) (1. Nf3 { Flexible and excellent, though it keeps your plans hidden a little longer. } 1... d5 2. d4 Nf6 { That settles into a quiet queen pawn game. Today the king pawn leads instead. }) 1... e5 { His pawn meets yours in the centre. Now play the move that gives this whole opening its name: the queen knight to c3. [%cal Yb1c3] } 2. Nc3 { The knight on c3 defends your pawn on e4, so the f pawn is free to charge forward later without leaving that pawn hanging. [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc3e4,Gf2f4] } (2. Nf3 { The most played move in all of chess, and a fine one. } 2... Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 { That pin is the Ruy Lopez, one of the deepest openings there is. We are learning the Viennas knight-first plan instead. }) (2. Bc4 { Developing the bishop first often reaches a similar position by another road. } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 Bc5 { Your knight and bishop land on the very same squares as our main line, though his bishop beat his own knight to c5 first. }) (2. d4 { The Centre Game rips the middle open at once, though it costs White a pawn. } 2... exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight attacks your queen on d4, so the tempo you spent capturing is handed straight back. }) (2. f4 { The Kings Gambit is the same spirit as the Vienna, just thrown two moves before your other pieces are ready. } 2... exf4 3. Nf3 g5 { He holds onto the pawn with g5, and your own king already feels the draught down the open f column. Develop a knight first instead. }) 2... Nc6 { He brings a knight out. Now aim your bishop at his weakest point, the pawn on f7. [%cal Yf1c4] } 3. Bc4 { The bishop on c4 stares straight down the diagonal at f7, the one square only his king defends. [%cal Gc4f7] } (3. Nf3 { Strong and completely natural too. } 3... Nf6 4. d4 exd4 { A fine, open game either way, but developing the bishop first keeps your f pawn free for later. }) (3. f4 { The gambit again, but too soon: his knight already sits on c6, not f6. } 3... exf4 4. Nf3 g5 { He simply keeps the pawn. This gambit wants his knight committed to f6 first. }) (3. g3 { The quiet Vienna: fianchetto the bishop and play a slower game. } 3... a5 4. Bg2 Bc5 { Perfectly sound chess, but today we came to put the bishop on c4. }) (3. Bb5 { The Spanish bishop, except there is no pin here, and his knight simply kicks it. } 3... Nd4 4. Nf3 c6 { His knight jumps to d4 attacking the bishop, and it ends up back on c4 anyway. }) 3... Nf6 { His knight eyes your pawn on e4. Do not defend it with a piece; a little pawn move does the job. [%cal Yd2d3] } 4. d3 { Solid, and it also means every one of his pieces now has to respect the f4 push still to come. [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf2f4] } (4. Nf3 { Natural, but count who is actually guarding your pawn on e4 first. } 4... Nxe4 5. Nxe4 d5 { His pawn on d5 forks your knight on e4 and your bishop on c4. Retreat the bishop, let him take the knight, then recapture the pawn: the material balances out again. }) (4. Bxf7+ { Never trade a whole bishop for one check. } 4... Kxf7 { He simply recaptures, walks his king home, and you are down a bishop for a single pawn. }) (4. Bb3 { A retreat nobody forced yet, spending a move on safety instead of development. } 4... Bc5 5. d3 a5 { Playable, but his pawn on a5 is already lining up to chase your bishop again with a4. }) (4. Bb5 { The Spanish idea again, and his knight answers it the same way. } 4... Nd4 5. Nf3 c6 { His knight jumps to d4 and hits the bishop, which ends up retreating to c4 regardless. }) 4... Bc5 { His bishop settles on c5. This is the moment the whole Vienna has been building toward: throw the f pawn forward. [%cal Yf2f4] } 5. f4 { The f pawn strikes at e5: a Kings Gambit played two moves late, except this time the knight on c3 already guards your pawn on e4. This is the soul of the Vienna. [%cal Gf4e5] } (5. Nf3 { Calm and completely sound, a respectable way to meet this position too. } 5... O-O 6. O-O h6 { White keeps a small, steady edge here, but the sharp f pawn push is the whole reason we chose the Vienna instead. }) (5. h3 { A useful precaution, ruling out a future pin on g4. } 5... Na5 6. Qf3 c6 { Solid and quiet chess for both sides. Today we are choosing the storm of f4 instead. }) (5. Bg5 { Pinning the knight on f6 first is the most popular try in this position. } 5... h6 6. Bh4 Be7 { Sound chess too, but our Vienna throws the f pawn at him rather than a pin. }) (5. a4 { The wing pawn only rules out a future ...a5 push — a precaution, not a plan. } 5... d6 6. Nf3 h6 { Safe for both sides, though far quieter than the f pawn charge we are here to learn. }) (5. Nd5 { The knight jumps into the hole, but his own knight is sitting right there to trade it off. } 5... Nxd5 6. Bxd5 Qh4 { Material stays level, but his queen has become the active piece, aiming down the row at your pawn on e4. The f pawn charge keeps the initiative for you instead. }) 5... d6 { He props up e5 and keeps the central tension alive. Finish developing with the king knight. [%cal Yg1f3] } 6. Nf3 { The Vienna set-up is complete: both knights and the bishop are out, two pawns back the centre, and the f pawn already leans on e5. [%csl Gc3,Gf3,Gc4][%cal Gf4e5] } (6. f5 { Clamping the kingside at once is just as good a plan, and a later chapter is built around exactly this push. } 6... Nd4 7. Nf3 c6 { That knight jump leaves things about equal and very playable. Here we finish developing before deciding on f5. }) (6. fxe5 { Trading off the central tension helps him more than you: it frees g4 for his knight to jump into. } 6... Ng4 7. exd6 Qxd6 { His knight and bishop on c5 now both bear down on f2, a square only your king still guards — suddenly you are the one defending. }) (6. Nd5 { That knight jump runs into the same answer as before: his knight simply captures it. } 6... Nxd5 7. Bxd5 exf4 { Your knight is gone, replaced by his bishop, and he has grabbed your f pawn in the bargain — you are down a pawn here. }) (6. h3 { The waiting move does nothing for your development, and while you spend it, he castles into safety for free. } 6... O-O 7. Nf3 a5 { Level material, but he is organised and safe while your attack has not even begun. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: he takes on f4"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/uoVYd808"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/2b1p3/2B1PP2/2NP4/PPP3PP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/2b1p3/2B1PP2/2NP4/PPP3PP/R1BQK1NR_b_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ He has just grabbed your f pawn. Only one piece can take it back, so the real work starts after that: make the half-open f column count. }
5... exf4 { His pawn takes yours on f4. Take it straight back. } 6. Bxf4 { The bishop takes back. Your f pawn is gone from its column, and that half-open column is where a rook belongs once you have castled. [%csl Gf4] } (6. Nf3 { Developing instead of recapturing at once is just as good. } 6... Ng4 7. Bxf4 Nf2 { His knight forks your queen on d1 and your rook on h1. Material stays level, but it is an ugly mess to untangle. [%cal Rf2d1,Rf2h1] }) (6. Bd2 { The long way round to f4 costs a whole extra tempo to recapture the same pawn. } 6... Nd4 7. Bxf4 c6 { He spends that free tempo planting a knight right in the centre on d4. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. h3 { A quiet pawn move while your king still sits in the middle of the board. } 6... d5 7. exd5 Nd4 { He rips the centre open and drops a knight into your position while your king has nowhere safe to go. [%csl Rd4] }) 6... d6 { He props e5 back up with the pawn. Now bring your last minor piece into the game. } 7. Nf3 { The strongest square for it. Every minor piece is out, and the half-open f column already points straight at his king. [%csl Gf3] } (7. Qd2 { The queen steps out, but your last knight is still asleep on g1. } 7... O-O 8. Nf3 Be6 { He is castled and comfortable, and you spent a whole move getting the queen out for nothing. }) (7. Bb3 { Tucking the bishop away before anybody has even asked it to move. } 7... Ng4 8. Qd2 Nf2 { Material stays level, but you have handed him that same knight fork for free. [%cal Rf2d1] }) (7. Bg5 { A pin that pins nothing: his knight on f6 is not defending anything it cannot give up. } 7... h6 8. Bh4 O-O { He kicks the bishop and castles safely. The pin achieved nothing at all. }) 7... O-O { He tucks his king away, finally caught up in development. } 8. d4 { The pawn returns to the centre and hits his bishop on c5, so you stand freer everywhere on the board. [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd4c5] } (8. Qd2 { Also fine, and the queen finds a genuinely useful square there. } 8... Be6 9. Bxe6 fxe6 { Material stays level here too, but pushing to d4 hits his bishop and wins a move outright. }) (8. Bb3 { The bishop steps back with nothing actually chasing it yet. } 8... a5 9. Qd2 Be6 { He grabs space on the wing for free while your bishop shuffles sideways. }) (8. Na4 { Hunting the bishop this way pulls your own knight clean out of the centre. } 8... d5 9. Nxc5 dxc4 { He rips the centre open and your knight ends up stranded far from the action. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: he castles early"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/YTC07ILj"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/2b1p3/2B1PP2/2NP4/PPP3PP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/2b1p3/2B1PP2/2NP4/PPP3PP/R1BQK1NR_b_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ The same Vienna set up, but this time he tucks his king away instead of taking your pawn. You already know where to aim your own attack. }
5... O-O { His king has picked its corner. Do not storm yet — one of your own pieces is still sitting at home. } 6. Nf3 { Well spotted. Finish your own development first; the pawn storm keeps for later. [%csl Gf3] } (6. f5 { The clamp, one move too early: your king knight is still in its box. } 6... Nd4 7. Nf3 b5 { His knight settles onto d4 for free, and nothing of yours is ready to chase it off. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. fxe5 { Trading off the tension does not develop anything, and it opens the door for a pawn strike back. } 6... d5 7. exf6 Qxf6 { His queen recaptures the knight, but look at your bishop on c4 — his pawn on d5 already attacks it, and saving it gives back most of what you just won. }) (6. Bb3 { A retreat that nobody has forced yet. } 6... d6 7. f5 Nd4 { That wasted move let his knight claim d4 for free, and you stand slightly worse here. }) (6. Bd5 { Planting the bishop in the centre puts it right where his knight can hit it next. } 6... exf4 7. Bxf4 Nd4 { Material is level, but that knight on d4 is a monster, and nothing of yours can challenge it. }) 6... d6 { He props e5 back up and holds the centre. Now the pawn on f4 is free to run past it. } 7. f5 { The clamp fixes his kingside pawns in place, and the g pawn is ready to follow it forward. [%csl Gf5][%cal Gg2g4] } (7. Na4 { Hunting the bishop first is just as good a plan. } 7... Bb6 8. Nxb6 axb6 { Trading knight for bishop keeps things equal and quiet. The clamp on f5 is the far more ambitious choice. }) (7. fxe5 { Letting the tension go lets his pieces breathe when they were the ones under pressure. } 7... dxe5 8. Na4 Bb4+ { His bishop gives check, and now you are the one who has to stop and solve a problem instead of attacking. }) (7. h3 { Too slow: he simply takes on f4 the moment it suits him. } 7... exf4 8. Bxf4 Be6 { His own bishop comes out to e6 unopposed, and he sits very comfortably here. }) 7... Nd4 { His knight jumps into the centre. Yours can jump further still. } 8. Nd5 { Trading favours the side with the attack, and d5 is the stronger outpost for your own knight to sit on. [%csl Gd5] } (8. Nxd4 { Trading right away is fine, just a quieter road. } 8... exd4 9. Nd5 Nxd5 { His knight recaptures on d5 too, but yours was guarded twice, so retaking keeps material level with far fewer pieces left to manage than trading on d4 gave you. }) (8. Bg5 { A pin that pins nothing, while his knight still sits unchallenged in the middle. } 8... c6 9. Na4 Bb4+ { A check again, and his knight still owns the d4 square throughout. }) (8. a4 { A wing move while an enemy knight sits deep in your own half of the board. } 8... c6 9. Nxd4 exd4 { You had to capture it anyway, a move later and with less to show for it. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the knight hunts your bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/cdtUCVVd"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p3/2B1P3/2NP4/PPP2PPP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 0 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p3/2B1P3/2NP4/PPP2PPP/R1BQK1NR_b_KQkq_-_0_4"]

{ Your bishop on c4 is the piece he fears most, so a knight heads to the rim to hunt it down. }
4... Na5 { The knight steps sideways after your bishop. Save it, or let the trade happen? Either way, the Vienna rule holds: your king knight never sits in front of the f pawn. [%cal Ra5c4] } 5. Nge2 { Out to e2, so the f pawn stays free to charge, and let him take the bishop: your d pawn takes back on c4 and joins the e pawn in gripping d5. [%csl Ge2][%cal Gf2f4] } (5. Bb3 { Just as good: he trades on b3, your a pawn takes back and the a column opens for your rook. We drill the knight move because it develops a piece instead. } 5... Nxb3 6. axb3 d5 { Level. Your rook on a1 already looks down a half-open column, and he strikes in the centre. A fine road, just not ours. }) (5. Nf3 { Playable, but the knight now sits in front of the f pawn, and the Vienna storm is off. } 5... Nxc4 6. dxc4 Bb4 { He trades and pins your other knight. Level chess, with your f pawn stuck at home behind the knight for good. [%csl Rf3] }) (5. Bb5 { The bishop runs, but there is no pin on b5 and the rim is no home for it. } 5... c6 6. Ba4 d5 { His c pawn kicks the bishop to the edge, then d5 strikes in the centre. Two bishop moves, and he has the bigger centre. [%csl Ra4] }) (5. Bg5 { The pin holds nothing, since his queen is not tied to that knight, and your bishop on c4 is still under attack. } 5... d6 6. Bb3 Be7 { You save the bishop a move late and he unpins in comfort. Level, and the pin cost you a tempo. }) (5. a3 { A useful little move that changes nothing about the trade to come. } 5... Nxc4 6. dxc4 d6 { The very same trade happens anyway, one move later. Material stays level and the position is pleasant. }) (5. Qf3 { The queen defends the bishop, a fine idea though a touch early. } 5... c6 6. Nge2 b5 { His pawn to b5 attacks your bishop on c4 directly, so it has to move again regardless. }) 5... Nxc4 { He trades his rim knight for your good bishop. Take it back. } 6. dxc4 { The d pawn takes back. Your pawns on c4 and e4 both watch d5 now, and the d column is open for your queen. [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc4d5,Ge4d5] } (6. f4 { The Vienna push at exactly the wrong moment: his knight is sitting on c4 waiting to be taken, and you leave it there. } 6... Nb6 7. fxe5 Nh5 { The knight ran off with your bishop in its pocket. A whole piece down for nothing. [%csl Rb6] }) 6... Bc5 { His bishop settles on c5. Now bring the queen to a square that does three jobs at once. } 7. Qd3 { She eyes d5, defends the pawn on e4, and steps off the back row, one move closer to castling long. [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd3d5,Gd3e4] } (7. O-O { Castling short is safe, sound, and just a little quieter. } 7... d6 8. Qd3 Be6 { The very same queen move arrives a move later. Nothing at all is lost by waiting. }) (7. h3 { A waiting move when the queen had a genuinely useful square waiting for it. } 7... d6 8. Qd3 Be6 { You reach the same square anyway, just a move behind where you could have been. }) (7. f4 { The pawn push looks thematic, but it hands his knight a lovely square. } 7... Ng4 8. h3 Nf2 { The knight dives into f2, forking your queen on d1 and your rook on h1 once again. [%cal Rf2d1,Rf2h1] }) 7... d6 { He holds the centre firm. Offer a trade of his best remaining piece. } 8. Be3 { The bishop offers itself for trade. Either way you answer, you are left comfortable. [%cal Ge3c5] } (8. O-O { Castling now is calm and good, just a shade less pointed than offering the trade. } 8... Be6 9. Ng3 O-O { Level and pleasant either way. The trade offer simply asks him a harder question first. }) (8. Ng3 { Heading the knight for f5 is also fine, just a slower route to the same kind of edge. } 8... Be6 9. O-O O-O { Equal, and his bishop on c5 keeps its good diagonal since nothing challenged it. }) (8. Na4 { Chasing the bishop with the wrong piece: this knight was needed elsewhere. } 8... Nd7 9. Be3 Bxe3 { The trade happens anyway through a different piece, and you have simply lost time getting there. }) 1-0


