[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK1NR_b_KQkq_-_3_3"]

{ If he copies your Vienna bishop instead of bringing his king knight out, that copying carries a real price. }
3... Bc5 { He mirrors your bishop move, but his king knight is still asleep at home. What has he left unguarded? } 4. Qg4 { Out she comes, staring straight down the open g column at his pawn on g7, and he has no comfortable answer. [%cal Gg4g7] } (4. Qh5 { The sister jump to h5 works just as well, hitting the same weak point. } 4... Qf6 5. Nf3 Nge7 { Two different roads to the same headache for him. Either queen move is fine. }) (4. Nf3 { Quiet, respectable development, with nothing wrong in it. } 4... Nf6 5. O-O O-O { That settles into a normal game, but the queen jump asks him far harder questions right now. }) (4. d3 { Solid and sound, and it keeps every one of your pieces defended. } 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 h6 { A pleasant, steady edge for you either way. The queen sortie is simply the sharper choice. }) (4. Nd5 { The knight jumps at the hole on d5, but nothing of yours is even attacking that square yet. } 4... Nf6 5. c3 O-O { He develops with a free tempo, and the jump has gained you nothing at all. }) 4... Kf8 { His best try: the king guards g7 himself and gives up castling for it. A free move for you, it seems. Look at his d pawn before you spend it. [%cal Rf8g7] } 5. Qf3 { She steps to f3, still aiming at f7, and out of the d pawn's reach: if he pushes to d5 now, your bishop simply takes it. [%cal Gf3f7] } (5. Qxg7+ { Look again before grabbing: his king walked to f8, and f8 defends g7. } 5... Kxg7 { The king simply recaptures the queen. You just lost the queen for a single pawn. [%cal Rf8g7] }) (5. Qg3 { Just as good, and every bit as pointed at his weak squares. } 5... Nf6 6. Nge2 h5 { Equally pleasant for you. Either queen square keeps her active. }) (5. Qh5 { She stays out on h5, but he can chase her from there too. } 5... Qf6 6. Nf3 g6 { His pawn hits the queen, and you lose the very tempo you had gained. }) (5. Qd1 { All the way back home. Safe, and it throws away everything the raid achieved. } 5... Qh4 6. Qf3 Nf6 { He seizes the initiative and his king is perfectly fine on f8. }) (5. Nf3 { A natural developing move, and it walks straight into his d pawn. } 5... d5 { The pawn hits your bishop, and behind it his bishop on c8 now stares at your queen. Two pieces attacked at once. [%cal Rd5c4,Rc8g4] } 6. Qg3 dxc4 { The queen ran, so the bishop fell. A whole piece gone for one pawn push. [%csl Rc4] }) (5. d3 { Solid looking, but it does nothing about the d pawn either. } 5... d5 { There it is: the pawn attacks your bishop and opens his bishop on c8 onto your queen. [%cal Rd5c4,Rc8g4] } 6. Qg3 dxc4 { She had to run, and your bishop was left to die on c4. A piece down. }) (5. Nge2 { The other knight square, and the same blind spot. } 5... d5 { The pawn hits c4 and uncovers his bishop against your queen on g4. [%cal Rd5c4,Rc8g4] } 6. Qg3 dxc4 { One pawn push cost you the bishop. Down a whole piece with nothing to show. }) 5... Nf6 { He finally develops. Bring your own knight out behind the queen. } 6. Nge2 { The king knight, leaving f3 free for the queen. Every one of your pieces still holds its post. [%csl Ge2] } (6. d3 { Solid, but it lets his knight leap into d4 completely unopposed. } 6... Nd4 7. Qd1 c6 { His knight attacks your queen from d4 and chases her all the way home to d1. }) (6. Qd1 { A retreat nobody forced. Now he is suddenly the one with the initiative. } 6... Nd4 7. Nf3 c6 { Three queen moves just to land back on d1, and meanwhile his knight claims d4 for free. }) (6. Bb3 { The bishop steps back while your own king still sits in the centre. } 6... Nd4 7. Qd1 d5 { He grabs the centre with tempo, and you stand clearly worse here. }) 6... Nd4 { His knight jumps at your queen. Trade it off before it settles for good. } 7. Nxd4 { Take it off at once — leave that knight alone and it comes to rule the whole board. [%csl Gd4] } (7. Qd3 { Stepping aside instead lets the knight simply stay where it is. } 7... a5 8. O-O c6 { His knight now sits in the centre for the rest of the game, and nothing challenges it. }) (7. Qg3 { Sidestepping looks safe, but count what his knight actually hits from d4 first. } 7... Nxc2+ 8. Kd1 Nxa1 { The knight forks your king and rook, then eats the rook on a1. You are lost, down a rook and a pawn for nothing. [%cal Rd4c2,Rc2a1] }) (7. Qxf6+ { A queen trade offer, except you are giving up the queen for a mere knight. } 7... Qxf6 8. Nd5 Qd8 { A whole queen traded for a knight. The game is effectively over. [%csl Rf6] }) 7... exd4 { He takes back, and his pawn on d4 now presses against your knight. } 8. Nd5 { Forward, never back: the knight jumps into the very hole his own pawn just made. [%csl Gd5] } (8. Ne2 { Retreating is safe, but the knight ends up doing nothing there. } 8... Qe7 9. c3 Qxe4 { He wins the pawn back with the queen and stands comfortably ahead. }) (8. Na4 { The rim again, and now your knight sits a long way from his king. } 8... Bd6 9. c3 Rb8 { Material is level at best, and your knight is stranded out on the edge. }) (8. e5 { A push that simply gives away the very pawn you were defending. } 8... dxc3 9. exf6 cxd2+ { His pawn crashes through with check, and the whole point of your set-up has gone. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the copycat queen raid"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/Cz1SyhDs"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P1Q1/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1B1K1NR b KQkq - 5 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P1Q1/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1B1K1NR_b_KQkq_-_5_4"]

{ The same Vienna copycat start, but this time his own queen comes out to play the hero. }
4... Qf6 { His queen guards g7 from f6 and glares down the column at f2. It looks as if f2 needs a guard. Does it, or is she the one in trouble? [%cal Rf6g7,Rf6f2] } 5. Nd5 { Forget f2: the knight jumps in and hits her on f6 and the pawn on c7 at the same time, so she has no time for anything else. [%cal Gd5f6,Gd5c7] } (5. Qxg7 { Wait before grabbing: his queen sits right there on f6, looking straight back at you. } 5... Qxg7 { She simply recaptures. You have just handed over your whole queen for a single pawn. [%cal Rf6g7] }) (5. Qg3 { A sensible retreat that keeps the game roughly level. } 5... Nge7 6. Nf3 Nd4 { Nothing wrong here, but you missed a far sharper answer on the board. }) (5. Qf3 { Offering a trade, though his queen was already the badly placed one. } 5... Nd4 6. Qxf6 Nxf6 { Trading queens here hands away your best attacking chance, and he is perfectly comfortable now. }) (5. d4 { Opening lines in the centre while his queen still sits on f6, exposed. } 5... d6 6. Qg3 Nxd4 { His knight grabs the pawn, and you stand worse for having let the moment pass. }) (5. Nh3 { The obvious guard for f2, and it walks into the d pawn trick from the last chapter. [%cal Gh3f2] } 5... d5 { His pawn hits the bishop on c4 and opens the bishop on c8 against your queen. [%cal Rd5c4,Rc8g4] } 6. Qg3 dxc4 { She ran and the bishop fell: a whole piece gone, for guarding a pawn that did not need it. }) (5. Nf3 { Development, but it does nothing for f2 and nothing about the d pawn. } 5... d5 { The push hits your bishop and uncovers his bishop on your queen at once. [%cal Rd5c4,Rc8g4] } 6. Qg3 dxc4 { A whole bishop lost to one pawn move. }) 5... Qxf2+ { She grabs the pawn with check. Let her: from here on she is the one running. } 6. Kd1 { The only legal move on the whole board. Step aside and smile. [%csl Gd1] } 6... Kf8 { He tucks the king away to hold g7. Now look at his queen on f2: she is deep in your camp with no friend near her. Which piece can go after her? } 7. Nh3 { The knight attacks her on f2, and she cannot go back the way she came: the knight on d5 watches f4 and f6. [%cal Gh3f2,Gd5f4,Gd5f6] } (7. Qxg7+ { Look again before grabbing: his king stands right beside that pawn. } 7... Kxg7 { The king simply eats the queen. That trade ends the game in his favour. [%cal Rf8g7] }) (7. Qf3 { Offering the trade lets her escape the whole net for free. } 7... Qxf3+ 8. Nxf3 Bd6 { Queens leave the board, and the pressure you spent three moves building has vanished with them. }) (7. Qe2 { The very same problem: you invite her to trade herself off safely. } 7... Qxe2+ 8. Nxe2 Bd6 { Material stays level, the queen walked out through the front door, and he is the one better placed now. }) (7. Nxc7 { A fork on his rook, but you let the queen on f2 go free just to grab it. } 7... d5 8. Qf3 dxc4 { His pawn scoops up your bishop on c4. Your knight does attack the rook on a8, but right now you are the one down material. }) 7... h5 { He hits your queen with the h pawn. He does not grab g2, because your queen looks straight down that column. Careful: taking his queen is not the answer either. [%cal Gg4g2] } 8. Qg5 { Out of the pawn's reach, still eyeing g7, and still covering g2. His queen has one safe square left, d4; he has an extra pawn, no castling, and nothing else out. [%cal Gg5g7,Gg5g2][%csl Gd4] } (8. Nxf2 { Taking her looks like the point of everything, but look at your own queen on g4 first. } 8... hxg4 { His h pawn takes her. Queen for queen. } 9. Nxg4 { You win the pawn back, and all the fire has gone out: level material and no attack. [%csl Rg4] }) (8. Qf3 { Offering a trade lets her walk out through the front door. } 8... Qxf3+ 9. gxf3 Bd6 { Queens off, your king side pawns are a wreck, and he keeps the extra pawn. He is better. }) (8. Qg3 { Same problem: she trades herself off at once. } 8... Qxg3 9. hxg3 Nf6 { Queens off and he is a clean pawn up with nothing to fear. }) (8. Qe2 { She steps back to e2, but he trades anyway. } 8... Qxe2+ 9. Bxe2 Bd6 { Queens gone, and his extra pawn is the only thing left to talk about. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the greedy knight"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/xRMDLtKP"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P1Q1/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1B1K1NR b KQkq - 5 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P1Q1/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1B1K1NR_b_KQkq_-_5_4"]

{ The same Vienna copycat idea, but this time a knight leaps forward instead of guarding home. }
4... Nd4 { The knight jumps into the centre but does nothing to protect the pawn on g7, still hanging to your queen. What is on offer? } 5. Qxg7 { The pawn falls for free, and now his corner rook is suddenly very nervous. [%cal Gg7h8] } (5. Bxf7+ { A check, but you are giving a whole bishop for one pawn. } 5... Kxf7 { He simply recaptures and walks his king home. The truly free pawn was sitting on g7 instead. [%cal Re8f7] }) (5. Bb3 { Saving the bishop from the knight works, but it is far too slow here. } 5... Nxb3 6. axb3 Nf6 { He trades the knight off anyway, and the free pawn on g7 is gone for good. }) (5. Nd5 { The other knight jump, except now his knight is the one causing you trouble. } 5... g6 6. Qd1 c6 { Your queen retreats with little to show for the whole detour. }) (5. Kd1 { Sidestepping a fork that was never actually there yet. } 5... Kf8 6. Qh5 Qe8 { A wasted move, and now his king sits right beside the pawn on g7, keeping it safe. }) 5... Qf6 { His best try: offer the queen trade to save the rook. } 6. Qxf6 { Take it, and stay a clean pawn up. Anything greedier than this goes wrong. [%csl Gf6] } (6. Qxh8 { The rook looks free, but count her escape squares in that corner first. } 6... Qxh8 { She is trapped and simply eaten. You gave a whole queen for a rook. [%cal Rf6h8] }) (6. Qg3 { Backing the queen off and leaving the rook alone entirely. } 6... Ne7 7. Nf3 Rg8 { He saves everything and stands slightly better, even a pawn down, with his knights so active. }) (6. Bxf7+ { A second bite at f7, and it costs you a bishop again. } 6... Qxf7 7. Qxe5+ Ne6 { Two pawns for a bishop is a poor trade, and your own king still sits undeveloped in the centre. }) (6. Qxf7+ { The other capture with check, except it is guarded twice over. } 6... Qxf7 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 { Queen traded for queen, then the bishop lost to his recapturing king — you end up the one slightly behind on material. }) 6... Nxf6 { He takes back with the knight. Before you develop anything, look at his other knight on d4: it is eyeing a fork on c2. [%cal Rd4c2] } 7. Bd3 { The bishop steps back to d3 and covers c2, so the fork never lands — and it is a developing move at the same time. A clean pawn up. [%cal Gd3c2][%csl Gc2] } (7. Nf3 { Natural enough, but look hard at what his knight on d4 is really aiming at. } 7... Nxc2+ 8. Kd1 Nxa1 { The knight forks your king and rook, then eats the rook on a1. The exchange, and more, is gone. [%cal Rd4c2,Rc2a1] }) (7. Bb3 { Also playable, though the pawn on c2 is still sitting there undefended. } 7... Rg8 8. Kf1 c6 { Material is level-ish, and your own king has lost the right to castle. }) (7. Kd1 { Defending c2 with the king itself works, but it is a clumsy way to do it. } 7... b5 8. Bxb5 Nxb5 { His knight wins back the bishop, and the extra pawn you were up disappears with it. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit accepted"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/X3YXFT9u"]
[Role "teaching"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]

{ His other main defence brings the king knight out first. This is where the real Vienna gambit lives. }
2... Nf6 { His knight attacks your pawn on e4 and defends e5 at the same time. Throw the f pawn at him anyway. [%cal Yf2f4] } 3. f4 { The Vienna gambit: give up the f pawn to rip the position open before he is ready for it. Sound or not, across the board it plays like a monster. [%cal Gf4e5] } (3. Nf3 { The calm road, and a very solid choice in its own right. } 3... Nc6 4. d4 Bb4 { Solid, sound chess for both sides — just not the reason we picked the Vienna today. }) (3. Bc4 { Developing the bishop first, heading straight for f7, is perfectly good too. } 3... Bc5 4. Nge2 O-O { A quiet game either way. The gambit is simply the sharper weapon we want. }) (3. g3 { The quiet Vienna, with a fianchetto still to come. } 3... Bc5 4. Bg2 O-O { Playable and pleasant, but today we came for the fireworks instead. }) (3. d4 { Straight down the middle, but he simply captures it. } 3... exd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight attacks your queen on d4, and the tempo you spent is handed straight back. }) 3... exf4 { He takes the bait. Now hit the knight before it can settle anywhere. [%cal Ye4e5] } 4. e5 { The whole point of the gambit. Every square his knight can run to is bad or useless. [%cal Ge5f6] } (4. Bc4 { Developing instead of pushing lets him consolidate at once. } 4... c6 5. d4 d5 { He keeps the pawn and builds a wall behind it. Your gambit has bought nothing at all. }) (4. Nf3 { Also natural, and also a move too slow for this position. } 4... d5 5. exd5 Nxd5 { He keeps the extra pawn and still gets a perfectly fine game. }) (4. d4 { A big centre, but you left his knight completely unbothered. } 4... Bb4 5. Bd3 d5 { The pin arrives, and the chance to play e5 yourself has gone for good. }) 4... Ng8 { All the way home. He owns one cold, extra pawn; you own the whole board. [%csl Rg8][%cal Yg1f3] } 5. Nf3 { Develop with a free tempo while his pieces still sleep at home. [%csl Gf3] } (5. Bc4 { The bishop belongs on this square too, but the knight gets there faster. } 5... d6 6. Qe2 dxe5 { He hands the pawn back and untangles. Less than you had before. }) (5. Qe2 { The queen props up the pawn, playable, though she blocks in your own bishop. } 5... Nc6 6. Nf3 g5 { He holds onto the extra pawn, and you are the one pushing uphill now. }) (5. Qf3 { Also playable, and again the queen takes the square your knight wanted. } 5... Nc6 6. Qxf4 d6 { Material comes back level. Developing the knight to f3 first keeps far more pressure on the board. }) 5... d6 { He strikes back at your proud pawn on e5. Answer by building the full centre. [%cal Yd2d4] } 6. d4 { Two big pawns and two knights out, while his pieces are all still at home. [%csl Ge5,Gd4] } (6. Qe2 { Also good, holding the pawn one move longer. } 6... dxe5 7. d4 Be7 { Playable, and the queen can even scoop e5 back later — building with the pawn to d4 simply does the same job with more menace. }) (6. exd6 { Releasing the tension yourself only frees up his game. } 6... Bxd6 7. d4 Ne7 { He develops with a free tempo, and the space you had has gone back to him. }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop again, though the centre was really the bigger prize here. } 6... Nc6 7. Qe2 dxe5 { He unwinds fully, and your grip on the position has loosened to dead level. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit main line"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
