{ Some players simply refuse the gambit outright. That caution hands you a pleasant game for free. }
3... d6 { He props e5 and declines the pawn. Do not grab it back yourself — what does this position actually want? } 4. Nf3 { Develop instead. The tension on e5 is yours to resolve whenever it suits you. [%csl Gf3] } (4. fxe5 { Capturing at once throws away the very tension that was working in your favour. } 4... dxe5 5. Bb5+ c6 { You released the tension for him, his pieces come alive with the check, and you stand worse. }) (4. Bb5+ { A check that only helps him improve his own pawn structure. } 4... c6 5. Be2 Nbd7 { The bishop has to retreat anyway, and he sits perfectly comfortable. }) (4. d4 { A big centre, but his pawn simply takes it and your queen comes out early. } 4... exd4 5. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight gains a tempo on your queen. Playable, but a little loose. }) (4. Be2 { Solid, quiet, and a shade slow for this position. } 4... d5 5. fxe5 Nxe4 { He strikes back in the centre before your own pieces are ready for it. }) 4... Be7 { He develops quietly. Finish bringing your own pieces out. } 5. Be2 { Modest and correct: castle quickly, then decide later what the f pawn should do. [%csl Ge2] } (5. d4 { Also good, and just as direct a way to play it. } 5... exd4 6. Qxd4 Nc6 { A fine game either way. Castling first simply keeps things calmer. }) (5. Bc4 { The usual Vienna square, except here it runs straight into a fork trick. } 5... Nxe4 6. Nxe4 d5 { His pawn forks both your bishop and your knight at once; retreat one, recapture with the other, and the material settles back level. }) (5. fxe5 { Releasing the tension again, and again a move too early. } 5... dxe5 6. Bc4 O-O { Level material, and you have handed back the free hand you had. }) 5... O-O { He tucks his king away safely. Do the very same. } 6. O-O { Castled, extra space, and the f pawn ready to open the column the moment you choose. [%cal Gf1f8] } (6. d4 { Grabbing the centre while your own king still sits in the middle. } 6... exd4 7. Nxd4 c5 { His pawn kicks the knight, and your own king is the one still exposed. }) (6. fxe5 { The same capture again, and it still helps him more than you. } 6... dxe5 7. O-O Nc6 { Level material. The half-open f column was worth more than that trade ever was. }) (6. d3 { Very solid, but you had a completely free move to spend better. } 6... exf4 7. Bxf4 c5 { Material stays equal, and he got to choose the moment the tension broke, not you. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: he ignores the gambit"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/q2gn8vjV"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{He ignores your gambit pawn on f4 and brings out a second knight instead. That lets you open the centre with a capture that also wins a tempo.}
3... Nc6 {His knight develops to c6, leaving the pawn on f4 completely alone. Capture on e5 now and make him spend a move winning it back.} 4. fxe5 {The capture is strong now: his knight on c6 has to leave its post to recover the pawn, so you come out a tempo ahead. [%csl Ge5]} (4. Nf3 {Quiet development lets him take back on f4 at his leisure, so the pawn you offered never really costs him anything.} 4... exf4 5. Bc4 Bb4 {He recaptures on f4 whenever it suits him, and you have nothing to show for the pawn you gave up.}) (4. Bb5 {The pin looks useful, but the pawn on e5 was there to be won first.} 4... exf4 5. Nf3 d5 {He grabs the pawn back and strikes in the centre with his own pawn to d5. The position is level at best.}) (4. d3 {Propping the pawn with a pawn move wastes the moment — the pawn on e5 was yours for the taking.} 4... Bb4 5. fxe5 Nxe5 { He settles in comfortably: by the time you take on e5 his knight is ready to take straight back. }) (4. Nd5 {The knight jumps to d5, defended by the pawn on e4, but it also leaves that same pawn without any other guard.} 4... Nxe4 5. Nf3 Nf6 {He grabs the loose pawn on e4 instead, and you are left both a pawn down and behind in development.}) 4... Nxe5 {His knight recaptures and sits in the middle of the board on e5. Push the pawn to d4 and kick it straight back.} 5. d4 {The pawn on d4 attacks the knight directly, and there is no square left where it stands comfortably. [%cal Gd4e5]} (5. Nf3 {This offers a trade rather than a second tempo — his knight is only too happy to come off on f3.} 5... Nxf3+ 6. Qxf3 d5 {He trades gladly and the position levels out completely. The direct pawn push kept far more pressure on.}) (5. Nge2 {Quiet development leaves his knight sitting comfortably in the centre on e5 for now.} 5... c5 6. d4 cxd4 {He gets time to prop the knight up with his own pawn to c5, and half of your advantage has gone with it.}) (5. Be2 {A modest developing move, but it hands him a free move to strike back in the centre himself.} 5... d5 6. d4 Ng6 {He gets his own pawn to d5 for free, and the position is close to level.}) (5. Bb5 {It looks like a pin toward your king, but his own pawn on d7 blocks the diagonal completely — the bishop is doing nothing at all.} 5... Ng6 6. d4 Bb4 {He gets his knight to safety and his bishop to an active square, and you have spent a tempo for nothing.}) 5... Ng6 {The knight retreats to the rim on g6. Push the pawn to e5 and kick the other knight, still sitting on f6, straight home.} 6. e5 {The pawn advances again, and this time it is his king's knight that has nowhere good to run. [%cal Ge5f6]} (6. Nf3 {Solid development, but it skips the second free kick that the pawn push to e5 was offering.} 6... d5 7. e5 Ne4 {He gets his knight to the strong square on e4 a move earlier than he should, because you let him strike in the centre first.}) (6. Bd3 {The bishop eyes the knight on g6, a fair idea, just slower than simply pushing the pawn.} 6... d5 7. e5 Ne4 {His knight settles on the strong e4 square all the same — exactly what the immediate pawn push to e5 was meant to prevent.}) (6. Qd3 {The queen comes out before your e pawn has finished chasing his last knight home.} 6... Bb4 7. Ne2 O-O {He pins the knight on c3 and then castles into safety. Your edge has shrunk to almost nothing.}) (6. Bc4 {The bishop heads for its favourite diagonal, but it is one move early — the pawn push to e5 was the more urgent job.} 6... c6 7. e5 d5 {You still get the pawn to e5 in, but his pawns on c6 and d5 close the centre and your attack slows right down.}) 6... Ng8 {Both his knights have been kicked backwards, one to g6 and the other all the way home. No tactic here: just a plain developing move that keeps both centre pawns guarded.} 7. Nf3 {Two big pawns stand in the centre, on d4 and e5, and both of his knights are still tangled up behind them. That lead is the whole reward for ignoring his gambit pawn. [%csl Ge5,Gd4]} (7. h4 {The rook pawn storms forward. It is a sharp try, just a wilder road than simply finishing development first.} 7... d5 8. h5 N6e7 {The knight retreats again and the position stays just as pleasant for you — developing first would have been the calmer road to the same place.}) (7. Bd3 {A fine square for the bishop, pointing straight at h7.} 7... d6 8. Qe2 dxe5 {The position stays comfortable either way — bringing the knight to f3 first simply keeps your initiative rolling a touch faster.}) (7. Qe2 {The queen adds support to the centre pawns. Nothing wrong with it, just less urgent than bringing the last knight out.} 7... Bb4 8. Bd2 Bxc3 { The trade is close to even, and it shows that the knight belonged on f3 all along. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the early bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/mk3IpsGO"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ He brings the bishop out before either knight. Different door, same house: your Vienna set-up does not change, but one trick along the way does. }
2... Bc5 { The bishop comes out early and stares at f2. Nothing changes for you: where does your own bishop belong in the Vienna? } 3. Bc4 { Bishop for bishop: yours stares at f7 just as his stares at f2, and the plan is the one you know — d3, then the f pawn. [%cal Gc4f7] } (3. Nf3 { A fine move that wins its share of games, but the knight sits down in front of the f pawn. That is the Italian, not the Vienna. } 3... d6 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 { A good open game for you, and not the set-up you are here to learn. }) (3. Qh5 { The queen raid, hitting e5 and f7 at once. } 3... d6 4. Bc4 Qf6 { He guards everything with a pawn and his queen, and yours is now a target. Playable, but the raid bought nothing. }) (3. f4 { The Vienna push a move too soon: with nothing on d3 or c4 yet, his bishop on c5 stares at the hole on f2, and he simply props e5 with a pawn. } 3... d6 4. Nf3 Bg4 { Level. The pin on f3 is annoying and your king has no safe home yet. Bishop out and d3 in first, then push. }) (3. Na4 { Chasing the bishop from the rim looks clever, and it walks into a sacrifice. } 3... Bxf2+ { He takes on f2 with check. [%cal Rc5f2] } 4. Kxf2 Qh4+ 5. Ke3 Qf4+ { The check on h4 dragged your king out, and now it is out in the middle of the board on move five. You are worse. [%csl Re3] }) (3. g3 { The quiet fianchetto is sound, but slow. } 3... a5 4. Bg2 Nc6 { Level, and the f pawn storm is a long way off. }) 3... Nf6 { His knight comes out and eyes your pawn on e4. Same answer as always. } 4. d3 { The little pawn move guards e4 and keeps the f pawn free to charge. This is the Vienna you know, with his bishop on c5 a move earlier than usual. [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd3e4,Gf2f4] } (4. Nge2 { Just as good: the knight comes out without blocking the f pawn. Playing d3 first simply keeps e4 guarded before the knight leaves g1. } 4... Nc6 5. O-O Na5 { His knight goes hunting your bishop, the same chase as in the knight-hunt chapter. Level either way. }) (4. Nf3 { Natural, and it puts the knight right in front of the f pawn. } 4... d6 5. d4 exd4 6. Nxd4 { A perfectly good game, but it is not the Vienna: the storm never comes. }) (4. f4 { The charge, one move too early: only the knight guards e4, and he hits back in the centre. [%cal Gc3e4] } 4... d5 5. exd5 e4 { His pawn on e4 shuts your centre and takes f3 away from your knight. You are worse, and the set-up is in pieces. [%csl Re4] }) (4. Bxf7+ { Never give a whole bishop for one check. } 4... Kxf7 { He takes back and walks his king home later. A bishop for a pawn. [%cal Re8f7] }) 4... d6 { He props up e5 with the pawn, and he does not take on e4: your d pawn would just take the knight back. Now, which pawn does the Vienna throw forward? } 5. f4 { The f pawn strikes at e5, with the knight on c3 guarding e4 behind it. The Vienna set-up is on the board again, by a different door. [%cal Gf4e5,Gc3e4] } (5. Na4 { Hunting the bishop is also good, but it is not the plan. } 5... Bb4+ 6. c3 d5 { He checks, you block, he strikes in the centre. Level and messy, and the f pawn is still at home. }) (5. Nf3 { Solid, but the knight blocks the f pawn: the whole point of the Vienna is gone. } 5... c6 6. O-O O-O { A quiet Italian-style game. Fine chess, and not what you came for. [%csl Rf3] }) (5. h3 { Stopping the knight jump to g4 is a fair idea, but it is a slow one. } 5... O-O 6. Nf3 c6 { Solid and quiet, and the f pawn is still waiting behind the knight. }) (5. Bg5 { The pin bites nothing while his king is still at home. } 5... c6 6. Nf3 Nbd7 { He unpins in comfort and you have spent a move on a bishop that will be kicked. He is a shade better. }) (5. Nge2 { The knight comes out without blocking the f pawn, but look where his knight can jump now. } 5... Ng4 { The knight on g4 hits f2, and only your king guards it. [%cal Rg4f2] } 6. d4 exd4 { You are already worse: your centre is falling apart while the knight on g4 hangs over f2. }) 5... Ng4 { His knight jumps to g4, eyeing a fork on f2. It looks like you must defend. Before you do, ask what is actually holding that knight on g4. [%cal Rg4f2] } 6. f5 { The pawn steps to f5 and shuts his bishop on c8 out of g4, so your queen stares at a knight nothing defends. Jump to f2 anyway, and queen to h5 threatens mate on f7 first. [%cal Gd1g4][%csl Gf5] } (6. Nh3 { The obvious guard for f2, and it loses on the spot. [%cal Gh3f2] } 6... Qh4+ { Check from h4, down the diagonal your f pawn left open. [%cal Rh4e1] } 7. Kd2 Nf2 { The fork lands anyway: queen and rook attacked, with your king already out on d2. [%cal Rf2d1,Rf2h1] }) (6. Nf3 { Development that guards nothing on f2: the fork lands. } 6... Nf2 7. Qe2 Nxh1 { Your queen stepped aside and the knight took the rook in the corner. A rook for a knight, gone. [%cal Rf2h1] }) (6. Qf3 { The queen guards f2, but the fork still hits your rook. [%cal Gf3f2] } 6... Nf2 7. Qh5 Be6 { Your rook on h1 is hanging, his bishop shuts your queen out of f7, and you are worse. }) (6. fxe5 { Taking on e5 lets him take on g1. } 6... Bxg1 7. Rxg1 Qh4+ { He took your knight, and now the check on h4 drags your king out with the rook stuck in the corner. [%cal Rh4e1] }) (6. Qe2 { The queen guards f2 from e2, but now the pawn on f4 hangs. [%cal Ge2f2][%csl Rf4] } 6... exf4 7. Nf3 Bf2+ { Check, and your king loses castling: his pieces are all over your king side. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the timid d6"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/dLwM07OS"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{Against the Vienna, when he refuses to fight for the centre at all, take every square of it and be glad to.}
2... d6 {A timid little move. It only guards the pawn on e5 and does nothing else at all. Seize the whole centre while you can.} 3. d4 {Straight ahead — he must now either give up the centre entirely or defend it a pawn short. [%csl Gd4]} (3. f4 {The gambit again, but here his pawn on d6 is already propping up the pawn on e5, so the sacrifice buys much less than usual.} 3... exf4 4. Qf3 Nc6 {He takes the pawn on f4 back and develops the knight to c6. You have handed him the whole centre for nothing.}) (3. Nf3 {Solid development. There is nothing wrong with it, it is only that the whole centre was there for the pawn to take.} 3... c5 4. Bc4 Be7 {A perfectly normal game. You turned down the free central space that d4 would have grabbed outright.}) (3. Bc4 {The bishop comes out first. Also playable, just a little less to the point than striking in the centre.} 3... Be7 4. Nf3 c5 {Roughly equal chess. The pawn to d4 asks a far harder question of his position than this does.}) (3. Nge2 {A quiet route for the knight, while the centre was still begging to be taken.} 3... Nf6 4. d4 c6 {You get the pawn to d4 in anyway, just one move later than you needed to.}) (3. g3 {The fianchetto. Solid enough, but it turns down the whole centre that was on offer.} 3... Nf6 4. Bg2 g6 {He gets his pieces out for free while you fianchetto, and the moment to grab the centre has passed.}) 3... Be7 {He tucks the bishop in quietly. Keep building your own centre with a natural developing move.} 4. Nf3 {The knight props up the pawn on d4 and eyes the centre at the same time. Every piece is doing its job. [%csl Gf3]} (4. dxe5 {Trading in the centre only helps him breathe more easily.} 4... dxe5 5. Qxd8+ Bxd8 {The queens come off and the position dries right up. You have gained nothing at all from the trade.}) (4. Be3 {Also good — the bishop backs up the pawn on d4 as a second guard, instead of the knight.} 4... Nf6 5. h3 c6 {Just as pleasant. Either move keeps the same grip on the centre.}) (4. d5 {Locking the centre with a pawn push throws away the freer, more active game you already had.} 4... Nd7 5. a4 Ngf6 {The position closes up, and his cramped pieces suddenly find the squares they needed.}) 4... Nd7 {His pieces are tripping over each other trying to get out. Bring your own bishop into the game.} 5. Bc4 {Every piece of yours sits on a good square, while he is still trying to untangle pieces across half the board. [%csl Gc4,Gd4]} (5. Be3 {Also fine, and just as strong — the bishop can stand guard over that pawn just as well as the knight.} 5... Ngf6 6. h3 c6 {Equally comfortable. Both bishop squares give the piece useful work.}) (5. h3 {A useful little move, if a slow one — nothing on the board was actually threatening you yet.} 5... c6 6. g4 h6 {Also playable, and a rather more committal way to spend the move.}) (5. a4 {Space on the queenside, when the centre is still where the real story is being written.} 5... Ngf6 6. Bc4 O-O {You bring the bishop out anyway, just one move later than in the main line.}) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the fake pin"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/J7TfBHsO"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ When his bishop aims at your knight and looks like a pin, check what actually happens if the bishop takes. It may not be a real pin at all. }
2... Bb4 { The bishop appears to pin the knight on c3 to your king. But your pawn on d2 is ready to take back there, so the pin has no bite. [%csl Gd2] } 3. Nd5 { Since the pin does nothing, the knight simply jumps forward, attacking the bishop on b4 and the pawn on c7 at the same time. [%cal Gd5b4,Gd5c7] } (3. d4 {Striking in the centre with the pawn is also strong, and every bit as playable as the knight jump.} 3... d6 4. dxe5 Bxc3+ {His bishop grabs the knight with check on c3. Compared to this, the immediate jump to d5 forces a much harder decision on him straight away.}) (3. a3 {Kicking the bishop with the pawn is playable, but he simply trades it for the knight at once.} 3... Bxc3 4. dxc3 d6 {Your pawns double up on the c column, and his bishop is gone for a knight — a fair trade, not a loss.}) (3. Nf3 {Quiet development, ignoring the bishop's threat completely.} 3... Bxc3 4. dxc3 d6 {He trades on c3 and doubles your pawns for free, since nothing made him think twice about it.}) (3. Qh5 {The early queen raid, aiming at the pawns on e5 and f7 together.} 3... Bxc3 4. bxc3 Qe7 {He holds everything together, and your pawns end up doubled on the c column — though the open lines and the bishop pair make up for it comfortably.}) 3... Bc5 { The bishop runs to a fresh diagonal instead of trading. His king knight is still at home, and you have seen in this file what that allows. } 4. Qg4 { The queen raid you already know: she hits g7, and his knight on g8 cannot help. [%cal Gg4g7] } (4. Nxc7+ { A fork on the king and the rook on a8 — but look hard at c7 before you jump in: something of his is watching that square. [%csl Rc7] } 4... Qxc7 { His queen was watching c7 the whole time and simply recaptures. You have given up a knight for one pawn. [%cal Rd8c7] }) (4. d4 { Striking in the centre is also strong, and he takes the pawn. } 4... Bxd4 5. Nf3 Nc6 { You are a pawn down for the moment, with a lead in development to show for it. Playable, but the queen raid costs nothing at all. }) (4. b4 { Kicking the bishop with the wing pawn is playable too. } 4... Be7 5. Nf3 Nf6 { He tucks the bishop away and develops. About equal, and your b pawn is loose out there. }) (4. c3 { Building slowly with the pawn to c3 is also fine, if a touch quieter than the raid. } 4... Nf6 5. d4 Nxd5 { He trades your good knight off, and the fake pin has cost him nothing. }) (4. Nf3 { Quiet development, and it lets him kick the knight straight back. } 4... c6 5. Nc3 Qb6 { The knight went to d5 and came home again, and his queen now eyes b2. Level at best. }) 4... Bf8 { All the way home: b4, c5, f8, three moves to end up where it started. Taking on f2 instead was no better: your king takes back and nothing follows. [%cal Rc5f8] } 5. Qg3 { Keep the queen active: from g3 she still eyes g7 and e5, and she is out of the way of your d pawn and bishop. [%cal Gg3g7,Gg3e5] } (5. Qxg7 { The pawn looks free, but it is not. } 5... Bxg7 { His bishop went home for exactly this. Queen for a pawn. [%cal Rf8g7] }) (5. Nxc7+ { The fork again, and again his queen guards c7. [%cal Rd8c7] } 5... Qxc7 { A knight for a pawn, and the raid is over. [%cal Rd8c7] }) (5. Nf3 { Also good, though he can offer a queen trade. } 5... d6 6. Qh4 Qxh4 7. Nxh4 { Queens off and his king breathes again. Keep her on the board instead. }) (5. d3 { Solid, and you play the queen to g3 a move later anyway. } 5... d6 6. Qg3 Nf6 { You reach the same kind of position a move later, with nothing lost. }) 5... d6 { He props up e5 with the pawn. One more piece out, and then count the score. } 6. Bc4 { Count the pieces out: your queen, knight and bishop are in play, and not one of his has left the back row. That is what the fake pin cost him. [%csl Gg3,Gd5,Gc4] } (6. f4 { The Vienna push works here too, and it is just as good. } 6... Nf6 7. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { A sharper game, and a fine one. The bishop move keeps every piece on the board and the lead in development plain to see. }) (6. Nf3 { Also fine. He develops with a hit on your knight and you trade it off; the bishop move keeps that knight where it is. } 6... Nf6 7. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { Level material and a pleasant game, with your best piece traded away. }) (6. Nxc7+ { The fork again, and his queen still guards c7. [%cal Rd8c7] } 6... Qxc7 { His queen takes the knight. A whole piece for a pawn. [%cal Rd8c7] }) (6. Bb5+ { A check that only helps him: his c pawn blocks it and kicks your knight in the same move. } 6... c6 7. Ba4 b5 { The bishop is chased to the rim and your knight on d5 is about to be taken. You are worse. }) 1-0
